In Java, Collection and Collections sound almost identical but serve entirely different purposes: Collection is an interface used to store and group data, while Collections is a utility class used to manipulate that data.
Think of Collection as the container where your data lives, and Collections as the toolbox used to sort, search, or modify the container.

Direct Comparison


Feature	Collection	Collections
What is it?	A foundational Interface.	A final Utility Class.
Purpose	Acts as a data structure to store and group individual objects as a single unit.	Provides helper methods to operate on or return data structures.
Inheritance	Extends the Iterable interface. It is the root interface for List, Set, and Queue.	Extends the Object class. It cannot be inherited or instantiated.
Method Types	Contains abstract, default, and static methods (since Java 8).	Contains only static methods.
Common Methods	add(), remove(), size(), clear(), contains().	sort(), binarySearch(), reverse(), shuffle(), max().


Code Example

Here is how both are used together in a Java program:
java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collection;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.List;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // 1. Using the Collection interface to store data
        // ArrayList implements List, which extends Collection
        Collection<Integer> numbers = new ArrayList<>();
        numbers.add(9);
        numbers.add(1);
        numbers.add(5);

        System.out.println("Original Collection: " + numbers);
        // Output: [9, 1, 5]

        // 2. Using the Collections utility class to manipulate the data
        // Note: The sort method requires a List reference
        List<Integer> numberList = (List<Integer>) numbers;
        Collections.sort(numberList);

        System.out.println("Sorted using Collections: " + numberList);
        // Output: [1, 5, 9]
    }
}
